      <h2 class="pdb-topic__heading">The Rise of Islam and Militant Islamism in Europe</h2>

      <p class="pdb-report__subject">Two Threats, One Continent: Jihadist Violence and Muslim Brotherhood Influence Networks Reshape European Security Policy</p>

      <p class="pdb-report__bluf">Militant Islamism is Europe's dominant terrorism threat in 2026 — responsible for 71 percent of counterterrorism arrests and all five terrorism-related deaths on the continent — while a concurrent Iran-backed proxy attack campaign and sustained Muslim Brotherhood legislative-influence operations are forcing European governments to adopt more assertive policy responses across the security, legislative, and immigration spectrums.</p>

      <!-- Background -->
      <div id="background">
        <p class="pdb-report__label">Background</p>
        <p class="pdb-report__bg">Europe hosts an estimated 26 million Muslims — roughly 5 percent of the total population — a figure that has more than doubled since 1990 through immigration and higher birth rates. The vast majority practice their faith without any connection to political Islam or violence. Militant Islamism, however — defined as religiously framed ideology that seeks to impose political and legal structures by force or subversion — has produced a sustained terrorism problem since at least 2001 and an intensifying influence-operations problem since the mid-2010s. Analysts and European governments now distinguish three overlapping threat streams: IS-inspired lone-actor and small-cell attacks, organized state-linked terrorism (primarily Iran-backed networks since February 2026), and "strategic Islamism" — the Muslim Brotherhood's long-term effort to build political influence through community organizations, schools, and media.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- Key Facts -->
      <div id="key-facts">
        <p class="pdb-report__label">Key Facts</p>
        <ul class="pdb-report__facts">
          <li><strong>Europol TE-SAT 2026:</strong> 24 completed jihadist attacks in the EU in 2025 (up from 21 in 2024). Of 486 total counterterrorism arrests, 347 (71%) were for jihadist offenses — the highest share since 2018. Five deaths and 81 injuries were attributed to jihadist attacks. 108 of the 347 jihadist suspects were under 18, consistent with a documented youth-radicalization trend.</li>
          <li><strong>Germany's Islamist scene:</strong> Germany's domestic intelligence service (BfV) assessed the domestic Islamist movement at approximately 28,000 adherents in its most recent annual report. Crimes attributed to religious ideology rose from 134 incidents in 2022 to 998 in 2024 — a more than sevenfold increase in two years — though definitional changes in reporting account for some of this growth.</li>
          <li><strong>Iran-HAYI proxy campaign:</strong> Since March 2026, the HAYI network — linked to Kata'ib Hezbollah and assessed as operating under Iranian direction — has conducted 17 or more attacks across the UK, Belgium, the Netherlands, France, Germany, North Macedonia, and Greece. Belgium's <em>Le Soir</em> reported approximately 150 Iran-linked incidents across Europe over the preceding five years. A key operative, Mohammad Baqer al-Saadi, was arrested in Turkey in May 2026 following joint European-Turkish intelligence cooperation.</li>
          <li><strong>Berlin Pride attack (25 July 2026):</strong> An IS-linked 21-year-old suspect killed one person and injured 29 at the Christopher Street Day parade. The suspect had been known to German intelligence and police but was released from pretrial detention in May 2026. The attack reignited German parliamentary debate on preventive detention for terrorism-assessed individuals.</li>
          <li><strong>Muslim Brotherhood legislative responses:</strong> France's National Assembly passed a resolution on 22 January 2026 (157–101) urging the government to designate the Muslim Brotherhood as a terrorist organization. The Dutch lower house passed a non-binding motion in March 2026 directing the government to examine Brotherhood networks. Germany's BfV issued a formal warning in its 2025 annual report on "strategic Islamism." Sweden's integration minister issued a public statement warning against political Islam in publicly funded community organizations.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- Points of Dispute -->
      <div id="dispute">
        <p class="pdb-report__label">Points of Dispute</p>
        <p class="pdb-report__dispute">
          <strong>Brotherhood designation:</strong> Whether the Muslim Brotherhood meets terrorism designation thresholds divides governments and analysts. Advocates for designation (France, Netherlands, Hungary) cite long-term subversion of democratic institutions. Critics (including many mainstream civil liberties and Muslim community organizations) argue designation would criminalize lawful political activity and produce backlash that complicates counterterrorism cooperation with Muslim communities. The EU has no common position.
          <br><br>
          <strong>Threat measurement — far right vs. jihadist:</strong> Left-leaning outlets and some academic researchers argue that far-right violence kills more people in Europe annually than jihadist attacks and that disproportionate counterterrorism resources skew toward Muslim communities. Right-leaning outlets and several European security services counter that Europol TE-SAT data consistently show jihadism producing more arrests, more plots, and comparable or greater lethality when mass-casualty attempts are counted.
          <br><br>
          <strong>Migration as radicalization driver:</strong> There is genuine empirical dispute over whether migration flows correlate with increased jihadist activity. Some European security officials (notably in Germany and Austria) have pointed to recent attackers' migrant backgrounds; civil liberties groups and migration researchers caution against conflating religion, migration status, and radicalization, noting that most attackers are citizens or long-term residents.
          <br><br>
          <strong>HAYI organizational coherence:</strong> European intelligence services differ on whether HAYI represents a centrally directed Iranian state operation or a looser network of ideologically aligned actors who receive facilitation and funding but operate with significant autonomy. The distinction matters for assessing whether disrupting al-Saadi and similar facilitators will degrade the campaign.
          <br><br>
          <strong>Islam vs. Islamism conflation:</strong> A recurring methodological and political dispute — evident across the source spectrum — concerns the degree to which coverage of violent Islamism bleeds into characterizations of European Muslim communities broadly. International outlets, particularly from Muslim-majority countries (Morocco, UAE), consistently flag this conflation as analytically imprecise and politically damaging to integration.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Watch -->
      <div id="watch">
        <p class="pdb-report__label">Watch</p>
        <ol class="pdb-report__watch">
          <li data-n="W1">
            <span>EU-level Muslim Brotherhood designation debate. France has requested an EU-wide discussion; a European designation would require consensus among member states with sharply divergent positions. The outcome will establish a legal and political precedent affecting counterterrorism financing law, civil society regulation, and EU-wide intelligence cooperation. <span class="pdb-report__assessment">Assessment: <strong>Likely to stall at EU level</strong> (moderate confidence) — member state division too wide for near-term consensus, though bilateral actions by France and the Netherlands may proceed independently.</span></span>
          </li>
          <li data-n="W2">
            <span>HAYI campaign trajectory. The pace and geographic spread of HAYI-linked operations since March 2026 suggest the network retains significant operational capacity despite the al-Saadi arrest. Watch for additional arrests, attack cadence, and whether operations shift from lower-profile harassment to higher-casualty targeting. <span class="pdb-report__assessment">Assessment: <strong>Campaign likely to continue</strong> (high confidence) — strategic incentive for Iran to maintain pressure while nuclear negotiations remain unresolved.</span></span>
          </li>
          <li data-n="W3">
            <span>German legislative response to Berlin Pride attack. The Bundestag is debating expanded preventive-detention authorities and stricter conditions for releasing terrorism-assessed individuals from pretrial custody. Legislative outcome will influence comparable debates in Austria, Belgium, and Sweden. <span class="pdb-report__assessment">Assessment: <strong>Moderate legislative action likely</strong> (moderate confidence) — coalition dynamics constrain the scope of any measure, but political pressure from the attack makes inaction difficult.</span></span>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>

